<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="50680" href="schedule/2018/spring/CMN/538/50680.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2018" href="schedule/2018.xml">2018</calendarYear><term id="120181" href="schedule/2018/spring.xml">Spring 2018</term><subject id="CMN" href="schedule/2018/spring/CMN.xml">Communication</subject><course id="538" href="schedule/2018/spring/CMN/538.xml">Seminar Rhetorical Theory</course></parents><sectionNumber>NO </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Classical Rhetoric</sectionTitle><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>This class consists of an in-depth introduction to the main outlines of the history of rhetorical theory in antiquity, with the bulk of attention given to ancient Greek rhetorical theory as it intersected with democracy, empire, and the rise of “philosophy.” Class readings (in translation) will focus on the Sophists, Plato, and Aristotle. Students will be expected to read, write, and discuss.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2018-01-16Z</startDate><endDate>2018-05-02Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>04:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>T      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>4103</roomNumber><buildingName>Lincoln Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="O'Gorman" firstName="T">O'Gorman, T</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>